     1	# Chapter 13 — The Reader
     2	
     3	They went down by the carts' street on the two hundred and ninety-first day, in daylight, in the open, with the boxes on a broker's handcart that Seln had borrowed for a copper and a look, because a working party that had been met in a cut at dusk was a working party that went home by the way everyone could see, and a thing everyone could see was a thing nobody could hold.
     4	
     5	He catalogued the change because a change of route was a change of state, and because Seln had made it without a word at first light, and the crew had taken it without a word, and the not-saying was the crew.
     6	
     7	Six of them, then, on a street with carts on it. Karis with the flat under her right arm and the left hand bound in a strip of Brom's shirt and her own thread, holding it against her coat as she held a thing she did not intend to use until it was mended and did not intend to mention until then either. Brom with the good shoulder carried carefully now, because he had used it wrong on a stave's man and it had told him so in the night, and the bound one carried as it had been carried since a marsh. Lira with two arms and a knife she had wiped twice. Vastin on the knee, with the stick, a day past a plane held across a cut, going up three tiers on it anyway, because a healer had said carry it to Millrace and the man had decided that a hand who could not carry was not a hand. And Cael with the satchel, and eleven things in him now, and the eleventh with nothing to do, because a plane across a cut was a thing you held when something was coming through it, and nothing was.
     8	
     9	The clerk counted four at the yard door and did not look at anyone's face for longer than a box's. He had looked twice. He had decided, Cael thought, not to look a third time, because a careful man who has looked twice at a thing and not asked has told himself a story about why, and does not want to be made to tell it a third time.
    10	
    11	Karis went up to the counter with Cleon as she had every morning, and came out by the yard door with the pass in his hand and her bound one held against her coat, and the clerk looked at the strip of shirt on it as he had looked at Brom's shoulder on the first morning — a careful man's eye going to the thing carried wrong — and did not ask, and Cael understood that the clerk had now not asked three things, and that a man who has not asked three things has a fourth in him somewhere, and that the fourth was a thing to be gone from the city before.
    12	
    13	Down. The iron door. The dark, and the lamp, and the man at the table who was already reading.
    14	
    15	He set the middle box against the rock where it went and stood by it, and Brom set the first box down and sat on it with the foot out, and Lira put the third box down and stood, and Vastin set the case down and did not sit, on the knee, because sitting was a thing he had decided a hand did not do until the reader did, and nobody had told him that and he had worked it out from six days of watching Cael, which was, Cael thought, the first thing the man had learned by watching rather than by being told, and was not nothing.
    16	
    17	---
    18	
    19	"Today I'll show you the inside of it," Cleon said, "because when I've finished the week I'm going to tell you a thing you'll not believe from the outside, and I want you to have seen how I see before I tell you what I saw."
    20	
    21	He said it to Karis, over the Hall's ninth sheet, on the sixth day of his week, with the board beside the sheet and the bound leaf of his own notes open on the other side, and Cael stood at the table's corner with the lamp a hand to the left of the sheet and held it still.
    22	
    23	"You've watched me read the bones," Cleon said. "Bearer, carried, ornament. That's the Path from outside — a man pointing at a wall. Now the inside. Sit where you can see the sheet and not my hand, because the hand's a habit and the habit's not the thing."
    24	
    25	Karis moved. Cael did not. He was labour, and labour held the lamp, and the lamp was two feet from the sheet and two feet from the man's hand, and he had held it there all week and was, he found, the person in the room best placed to watch a thing from inside, because the man had stopped seeing him.
    26	
