     7	The two hundred and ninety-third day, at the bell. Up the cellar stair and out by the yard door past a clerk who counted four and did not ask a fourth thing, and down the carts' street in the last light with the boxes on a borrowed handcart, and along the ring road to the inn, and into the yard behind it, where Seln had already lit the brazier, because Seln had been above all day and had read the crew's faces coming down the street from the yard gate the way he read anything coming toward him, and had lit a fire before anyone asked for one, because a fire was what this crew lit when a thing had to be said and the inn's hearth was a public one.
