     9	He had told Seln on the street, because Seln had been above and had not heard the leaf, and a thing that is going to be said at a fire is a thing the man at the gate should have before the fire is lit, so that he can face the road while it is said. He had told him in the fewest words a thing could be told in, walking, at the handcart's tail, and Seln had listened as he listened to a report, and had said, at the end of it, "Made," and then, "For a kind," and then nothing, and had gone ahead to the yard and had the brazier lit before the cart was through the gate.
    10	
    11	He catalogued the fire because it was going to be the place, and a place should be had before the thing that happens in it.
    12	
    13	The inn's yard, walled on three sides, a shed on the fourth, empty. A brazier on legs, iron, with the inn's kindling in it and a broker's broken crate on top of that, burning low. Six of them round it, and the seventh not there, on a north road, with a cup that Lira had brought out from the long table and set on the wall at the far end where a healer sat when she sat. Karis on an upturned bucket with the notebook shut in her lap and her bound hand on it and her face the colour of the ninth day of a week that had been asked for as seven, sitting the way a woman sits who has stood up from a table for the first time in ten hours and has found that her legs have opinions. Brom on the shed's step with the foot out. Seln at the yard gate, facing the ring road, because the ring road was where a thing would come from. Vastin on the wall, on the knee, with the stick across his knees. And Cleon.
    14	
    15	Cleon had come up.
    16	
    17	That was the thing Cael catalogued twice, because it was the thing he had not expected. The man had never once, in nine days, left his table before the bell, and had never once come up the stair with the party, and had never once been seen above ground by any of them except on a stair at noon going to a table on the third tier to eat alone; and he had come up with them tonight, at the bell, with the leaf left on his shelf under the registry and nothing in his hands, and had walked down the carts' street behind the handcart like a man who did not know how to walk behind a handcart, and had come into the yard, and had stood at the brazier's edge in a coat that had been good once, and had said nothing, and nobody had asked him why, because the why was on a leaf.
    18	
    19	Cael took the notebook from Karis. She gave it to him without opening her eyes. He opened it to the page — the passage in the small square hand; the patch drawn as a patch, with its one upright stroke; the three words under it — and crossed the yard to Lira, at the brazier's far side, and put it in her hands, open, and did not say anything, and stepped back.
