    26	
    27	She read it the way she did everything, which was fast, and then she read it again, which she did not do with anything, and her face did not change, because Lira's face did not change when a thing mattered; it changed when a thing did not. She read the three words at the bottom. She looked at the patch drawn as a patch. She closed the notebook, and held it a moment, and handed it back across the brazier, and he took it, and she looked at him — not the count; not a finding; the look she had given him in a hall at sixteen when a marshal had gone inside to read a page — and said the one true thing, which was not comfort, because Lira had never once in her life mistaken comfort for the truth.
    28	
    29	"So the whole time they weren't afraid you'd break something. They were afraid you'd *exist*."
    30	
    31	The fire took the next few seconds, and nobody gave them to anything else.
    32	
    33	He had known, coming up the stair, that she would say the true thing, and had not known what the true thing was, and now he had it, in nine words, from the person who had always been first, and it was worse than the leaf and it was better, because the leaf was a record and this was Lira, and a record sits and Lira did not.
    34	
    35	"Yes," he said.
    36	
    37	"Right." She put her two hands at her sides. "Then that's what it is."
    38	
    39	That was the whole of Lira's part, and it was, from Lira, the whole of the thing.
    40	
