    42	
    43	The circle, then, because that was the order: Lira first, and then the circle, and the circle took a thing in the order it took things, and had done since a hall.
    44	
    45	Brom priced it. He did it from the shed's step with the foot out, looking at the fire and not at anyone, at the length of a ledger line, which was how Brom said a thing that had cost him money and would cost him more. "So it's not that they didn't have a column for him." He turned it over once. "It's that the whole sheet was drawn so there couldn't be one." He was quiet. "That's a bad sheet. I've turned down a real job on the strength of it being a bad sheet, and it's worse than I priced it." He did not say anything else, and did not need to, because Brom had said the thing in the ledger's terms and the ledger's terms were the ones the crew kept.
    46	
