    50	
    51	She said it with her eyes shut, from the bucket, with her hand on the notebook that Cael had given back to her: "Two pages. The leaf says a man made the apparatus and why. A dead surveyor says a hundred lamps go out on one wire. I'm not drawing a line. I've said it in a hole and I'll say it at a fire. I've a year and a week of not-guessing behind me and I'm not going to spend it tonight because I'm tired." She opened her eyes and looked at Cael, once, across the brazier. "And I'm not going to say the third word again. I said it once, to the leaf. It's yours now. Do what you want with it." And she shut her eyes, and was, he thought, asleep before the sentence had finished being in the air, sitting up, on a bucket, with her hand on a book, which was a thing he had seen her do at a marsh and had not seen since.
    52	
    53	Cleon said nothing.
    54	
